    cons: [
      'Safety is a concern — beach theft and property crime are real issues',
      'Higher cost of living than Central Valley — tourist pricing on everything',
      'Limited healthcare — serious issues require travel to Liberia or San José',
      'Very hot and humid — not everyone can handle year-round tropical heat',
      'Rainy/green season — many businesses close, less to do',
      'Can feel small/isolated — not a big city with lots of cultural options',
      'Limited public transit — you really need a car',
      'Tourist crowds in high season — town gets busy and prices go up',
      'Internet is OK but not as reliable or fast as San José',
      'Limited school options for families with children',
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: 'Is Tamarindo a good place to live as an American expat?', a: 'It depends on what you want. If you love the beach, surfing, warm weather, and a laid-back outdoor lifestyle, Tamarindo is fantastic. It has a great expat community, lots of activities, and English is widely spoken. But if you prefer city life, culture, fine dining, or serious career opportunities, you would probably be happier in San José. Tamarindo is a small beach town — it is charming but can feel isolated at times. Many expats split their time or visit for extended periods rather than living there year-round.' },
      { q: 'How expensive is it to live in Tamarindo?', a: 'Tamarindo is more expensive than most of Costa Rica due to tourism and demand. A comfortable single lifestyle runs about $1,800–3,000 per month. Rent is the biggest variable — beachfront places are expensive, but you can find cheaper options a few blocks inland or in nearby towns like Huacas. The biggest expenses are rent, groceries (especially imported food), and transportation. It is cheaper than most US coastal towns but more expensive than inland Costa Rica.' },
      { q: 'Can I really work remotely from Tamarindo?', a: 'Yes — Tamarindo has become one of the top digital nomad destinations in Latin America. There are several co-working spaces with reliable internet and a big community of remote workers. Internet speeds are generally 50–100+ Mbps in most places — good enough for video calls and most remote work. Costa Rica\'s digital nomad visa makes it officially possible to stay long-term. The main trade-off is that you are living in a beach town — it can be hard to be productive when the ocean is calling your name.' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between high season and green season?', a: 'High season (dry season) runs roughly December through April. It is sunny, dry, and the busiest time for tourists — prices are highest, the town is full, and everything is open. Green season (rainy season) runs May through November. It rains most afternoons but mornings are often sunny. Many businesses close or have reduced hours. Prices drop significantly — you can find great deals on rent and activities. Some expats prefer green season because it is quieter and cheaper, and the rain is usually just a few hours a day.' },
      { q: 'Is Tamarindo safe?', a: 'Tamarindo is generally safe for tourists and expats but not crime-free. Petty theft is the main issue — beach theft, car break-ins, and occasional home burglaries. Violent crime against expats is rare. The safety situation has deteriorated somewhat as the town has grown rapidly, but it is still a relatively safe place to live if you take normal precautions. Most expats feel safe day-to-day but take security seriously with their homes and belongings.' },
      { q: 'How do I get to Tamarindo from the US?', a: 'The easiest way is to fly to Daniel Oduber Quirós International Airport in Liberia (LIR) — about a 1-hour drive from Tamarindo. Many US cities have direct flights to Liberia, especially in high season. You can also fly to Juan Santamaría International Airport (SJO) near San José — about a 4–5 hour drive from Tamarindo. From either airport you can rent a car, take a pre-booked shuttle, or take a public bus. Most expats rent or buy a car once they arrive.' },
    ],
