      note: 'Germany has a high tax burden, especially when including social security contributions. Combined income tax + social security can reach about 40–45% for middle earners (employee share only). However, these taxes fund world-class healthcare, nearly free university education, excellent infrastructure, generous parental leave (Elterngeld), and a strong social safety net. The solidarity surcharge was reduced and now only applies to higher earners. Church tax is optional — you can opt out by formally leaving the church. US citizens must file US taxes and use the foreign tax credit (German rates are high so FTC typically covers most US liability).',
