      tourist: {
        duration: '90 days visa-free (Schengen Area — Norway is part of Schengen)',
        eligible: 'US citizens: 90 days within any 180-day period in Schengen — no visa needed',
        note: 'Standard Schengen rules. Easy for tourism and short business trips. Norway is in the Schengen Area despite not being in the EU.',
      },
      digitalNomad: {
        duration: 'Digital nomad visa (introduced 2024)',
        income: 'NOK 35,000+/month income required (plus valid health insurance)',
        desc: 'Norway introduced a digital nomad visa in 2024. Allows remote workers to live in Norway for up to 2 years. Must show sufficient income from employment abroad or own business. Must have valid health insurance. Can bring family members. A new and potentially very attractive option for digital nomads who want to live in Norway.',
      },
      goldenVisa: {
        duration: 'Permanent residency after 3–5 years (depending on visa type)',
        desc: 'No golden visa program in Norway (the country is generally opposed to investment-for-residence schemes). Permanent residency is obtained through employment or other long-term visas. Skilled worker visa leads to permanent residency after 4 years. Family reunification is included.',
        note: 'Norway does not have a golden visa or citizenship-by-investment program. The country takes a principled stance against selling residency/citizenship. Permanent residency is earned through work and integration. Citizenship after 7 years of residency.',
      },
      work: {
        duration: 'Skilled worker visa — employer-sponsored',
        desc: 'The standard work visa for non-EEA citizens. Employer must offer a job at the prevailing wage. Must have relevant education or work experience. UDI (Norwegian Directorate of Immigration) processes applications. Permanent residency after 4 years.',
        process: 'Employer offers job. You apply online through UDI. You may need to provide proof of qualifications, income, and accommodation. Processing can take 1–3 months. Upon arrival, you get a residence card. Note: UDI processing times can be long — apply well in advance.',
      },
      retirement: {
        duration: 'Retirement visa — limited options',
        income: 'Must demonstrate sufficient pension income + health insurance',
        desc: 'Norway does not have a specific retirement visa program. Retirees from non-EEA countries generally need to apply through family reunification or other routes. Norway is not a typical retirement destination due to the high cost of living, cold climate, and lack of retirement visa programs.',
      },
      note: 'Norway offers several good visa options for skilled workers. The skilled worker visa is the most common route. The new digital nomad visa (2024) is an exciting addition. Norway is not in the EU but is in the EEA and Schengen Area. UDI (Directorate of Immigration) handles applications — processing can be slow. Permanent residency after 3–4 years depending on the visa type. Citizenship after 7 years of legal residency. Language requirements apply for citizenship (Norwegian B1 level).',
