      peak: '8:00–10:00 AM and 4:00–7:00 PM. Worse during holiday seasons (July–Aug, Dec–Jan). Beach road traffic can be terrible.',
      traffic: 'Moderate to heavy — traffic has gotten worse as Bali grows. Congestion in southern Bali is a growing problem. Scooters can weave through traffic but accidents are common.',
      best: 'Living and working in the same area. Many digital nomads work from co-working spaces near where they live. Avoiding peak travel times. Scooter is faster than car.',
      note: 'Traffic in Bali has gotten significantly worse in recent years as tourism and development have grown. Southern Bali (Canggu, Seminyak, Kuta, Uluwatu) can have very bad traffic — especially during peak hours and high season. Scooters are the fastest way to get around since they can weave through traffic. However, scooter accidents are common and safety is a concern. Many expats live and work in the same neighborhood to minimize commuting. Gojek and Grab are very popular for those who do not want to drive themselves. The island is relatively small — you can drive from one end of the south to the other in about 1–1.5 hours (depending on traffic). For trips to Ubud from the south, it takes about 1–1.5 hours. Overall, commuting on Bali requires patience and a scooter is the most practical way to get around.',
