      traffic: 'Moderate to heavy — traffic is bad for a city of its size. The narrow valley geography limits east-west routes. Congestion is getting worse.',
      best: 'Living close to work. Living in the same general area as your workplace. The Trolebús is good for north-south trips. Taxis are cheap but traffic increases fare.',
      note: 'Traffic in Quito is moderate to heavy — worse than you might expect for a city of 2 million. The city is long and narrow (north-south) which creates bottlenecks for east-west travel. Main avenues can be very congested at rush hour. The Trolebús (trolleybus BRT) is the fastest way to travel north-south through the city. Buses cover most routes but are crowded and confusing for foreigners. Taxis are very cheap — most rides within the city cost $1–3 — but traffic can make them slower. Uber and Cabify are available and often safer. Many expats choose to live close to work to minimize commute time. Living in Cumbayá (the upscale suburb east of the city) means a longer commute to central Quito. The Mariscal Sucre International Airport is about 45 minutes to an hour east of the city depending on traffic. Overall, commuting is manageable but traffic is a growing concern.',
