      public: 'The Dutch healthcare system is based on mandatory private health insurance with public regulation. Everyone must have health insurance. Basic insurance is standardized and covers essential care. Quality is very high and wait times are short.',
      private: 'Private supplementary insurance is available for dental care, physiotherapy, alternative medicine, etc. Most people have basic public insurance plus supplementary private coverage. All hospitals are privately run but regulated.',
      insurance: 'Mandatory for all residents. Basic health insurance costs €100–150/month per adult. Children are free. Employers do not provide insurance — you choose your own insurer. Many insurers compete. The government sets the basic package.',
      cost: 'Expensive insurance premiums but very good quality. Out-of-pocket costs are moderate. The deductible (eigen risico) is €385/year for adults. No extra costs above deductible for covered care except for supplementary treatments.',
      emergency: '112 for emergencies (same number across EU). Excellent ambulance service. Academic Medical Center (AMC) and VUmc are the main university hospitals. All hospitals have 24/7 ERs. Emergency care is excellent.',
      note: 'Healthcare in Amsterdam (and the Netherlands) is excellent — consistently ranked among the best in Europe and the world. The system is based on mandatory private health insurance with government oversight. Everyone must have basic health insurance, which covers all essential care. The quality of care is very high and wait times are short. Dutch doctors are well-trained and speak excellent English. The system is more focused on general practitioners (huisarts) as gatekeepers — you see your GP first for most issues, and they refer you to specialists. This can sometimes feel slow but it keeps the system efficient. Out-of-pocket costs are limited by the annual deductible (eigen risico) of €385. For expats, the healthcare system is easy to navigate — most doctors and specialists speak fluent English. Dental care is not covered by basic insurance and can be expensive. Overall, the Dutch healthcare system is world-class.',
