    healthcare: {
      rating: '6.5 / 10',
      system: 'Developing healthcare system with a growing private sector. Public healthcare is basic and crowded. Private healthcare in HCMC is good and very affordable. Several international-standard hospitals. Vietnam is a growing medical tourism destination.',
      public: 'Public healthcare is available but quality varies. Public hospitals can be very crowded and underfunded. Not recommended for expats who can afford private care. Long wait times and language barriers.',
      private: 'Good and growing private healthcare sector. Several international-standard hospitals with English-speaking doctors. Top hospitals: FV Hospital (Franco-Vietnamese), City International Hospital, Gia Dinh International Hospital, Hoan My hospitals. Modern facilities, shorter wait times, and affordable prices. FV Hospital is considered the best for expats.',
      insurance: 'Health insurance is not mandatory for visitors but highly recommended for expats. Many expats have international health insurance. Local private insurance is also available and affordable. Employer-sponsored insurance is common for professional expats.',
      cost: 'Very affordable. Private consultations $20–50. Procedures and hospital stays cost a fraction of Western prices. Dental work is very cheap and decent quality. Vietnam is a growing medical tourism destination (especially dental work and cosmetic surgery).',
      emergency: '115 for emergencies. Ambulance services vary in quality. For emergencies, expats go directly to private hospitals like FV Hospital. Most private hospitals have 24/7 ERs. Medical evacuation to Singapore or Bangkok is an option for very serious cases.',
      note: 'Healthcare in Ho Chi Minh City has improved significantly in recent years but is still not on par with Western countries. The private healthcare sector is growing rapidly and several hospitals offer international-standard care at affordable prices. FV Hospital (Franco-Vietnamese) is generally considered the best hospital for expats — it has international accreditation, English-speaking doctors, and modern facilities. City International Hospital and others are also good options. Public hospitals are crowded and not recommended for expats. For routine care, minor illnesses, and basic procedures, private clinics and hospitals in HCMC are fine and very affordable. For more complex or serious conditions, some expats prefer to go to Singapore or Bangkok (both about 1.5–2 hours by flight) for higher-level care. Dental work is very cheap and decent quality — Vietnam is a popular dental tourism destination. Pharmacies are common and many medications are available without a prescription. Overall, the private healthcare system is adequate for most expat needs and very affordable — but you need good insurance for anything serious.',
    },
