    taxes: {
      overview: 'Territorial tax system — only income earned in Panama is taxed. Foreign-source income is not taxed. Personal income tax up to 25%. This is a major advantage for expats and digital nomads with foreign income.',
      income: 'Progressive personal income tax: 0% to 25% top rate (on income above ~$50,000). Only Panama-source income is taxed. Foreign-earned income is tax-free (territorial system). Very favorable for expats with foreign income.',
      capitalGains: 'Capital gains tax: generally 10% for securities and 10% for real estate (in some cases). Lower than many countries. Tax treatment of investments is favorable.',
      property: 'Property tax: up to 2.1% of assessed value (with exemptions for lower-value properties). There is a 20-year property tax exemption for new construction (a big incentive for foreign buyers).',
      sales: 'ITBMS (VAT): 7% standard rate. Lower rate of 0% for some essential goods. Moderate compared to other countries.',
      corporate: 'Corporate income tax: 25% standard rate. Very competitive for Latin America. The territorial tax system means foreign-sourced income of Panamanian companies is not taxed — offshore companies benefit greatly.',
      socialSecurity: 'Social security (CSS): employee 9.75%, employer 12.25% (total ~22%). Includes pension, health, and disability. Employed expats contribute. Some professions have different rates.',
      note: 'Panama has one of the most favorable tax regimes in the world for expats, especially those with foreign income. The territorial tax system means that only income earned in Panama is taxed — foreign-source income is completely tax-free. This is a huge advantage for digital nomads, remote workers, retirees, and anyone earning income from outside Panama. Personal income tax rates are progressive with a top rate of 25% — lower than the US and most European countries. For US citizens, you still need to file US tax returns. The Foreign Earned Income Exclusion (FEIE) can exempt up to ~$126,500 (2024) of your earned income from US tax. Since Panama has relatively low tax rates, the Foreign Tax Credit may not fully offset US tax for high earners. The Friendly Nations Visa (Pensionado Visa and others) also offer tax incentives. The 20-year property tax exemption for new construction is another major benefit for real estate investors. Overall, the tax system is very favorable for expats, especially those with foreign-source income.',
    },
