      cards: 'Debit and credit cards widely accepted. Almost all stores and restaurants accept cards. Contactless payments becoming more common. Cash still used for small transactions. US dollar makes everything easy for Americans.',
      online: 'Good online and mobile banking at major banks. Most banks offer digital services. You can do most transactions online. International transfers are easy but fees vary. Online banking is generally reliable.',
      note: 'Panama has a large and sophisticated banking sector — it is an international banking hub with over 100 banks. Banco General and Banistmo are among the largest and most popular with expats. International banks like HSBC and Scotiabank also have a presence. Opening a bank account as a foreigner has become more regulated in recent years due to international pressure (FATCA, etc.), but it is still very doable with the right documentation. Requirements typically include passport, second ID, bank reference, proof of address, and proof of income/source of funds. Some banks are more expat-friendly than others. Since Panama uses the US dollar, there is no currency exchange for Americans — this is a huge convenience. Online and mobile banking are available at most major banks and are generally good. Wise and other international services are also popular for transfers. Panama has a reputation for banking privacy, though international transparency has increased. Overall, banking in Panama is modern, well-developed, and convenient — especially since the currency is US dollars.',
