    safety: {
      score: 55,
      summary: 'Generally safe for tourists and expats. Petty theft is the main concern. The country has made great progress since the civil war ended in 2009. Use normal precautions. Economic crisis has led to some social unrest.',
      violentCrime: 'Low-Medium — violent crime against foreigners is relatively rare. The civil war ended in 2009 and security has improved dramatically. However, petty crime and occasional violent incidents occur. The 2019 Easter bombings were a tragedy but an isolated event. Security is generally good.',
      pettyCrime: 'Medium — pickpocketing and theft occur in crowded areas, on public transport, and in tourist spots. Keep valuables secure. Snatch theft (by motorbike) is a known issue in some areas — keep bags on the side away from traffic.',
      nightSafety: null,
      scams: null,
      note: 'Colombo is generally safe for tourists and expats — violent crime against foreigners is relatively rare. The city and country have made significant security improvements since the end of the civil war in 2009. However, petty theft (pickpocketing, bag snatching) does occur, especially in crowded areas like Pettah Market, bus stations, and tourist spots. Snatch theft by motorbike is a known issue — keep bags on the side away from traffic and be careful with phones. Tourist scams (gem scams, textile shop scams, tuk-tuk scams) are common — be wary of friendly strangers offering great deals. Use PickMe or Uber instead of negotiating fares with tuk-tuk drivers. The 2019 Easter Sunday bombings were a tragic event but security has been greatly increased since then. The economic crisis of 2022 led to protests and some unrest, but the situation has stabilized. Natural hazards include monsoon flooding in some areas. Overall, most expats feel safe in Colombo with normal precautions — just be aware of your surroundings and take standard safety measures.',
    },
    healthcare: {
      rating: '6.5 / 10',
      system: 'Developing healthcare system with a growing private sector. Public healthcare is basic and underfunded. Private healthcare in Colombo is decent and very affordable. Several private hospitals with good standards.',
      public: 'Public healthcare is free for all citizens but quality varies. Public hospitals can be very crowded and underfunded. Not recommended for expats who can afford private care. Long wait times.',
      private: 'Decent and growing private healthcare sector. Several private hospitals with modern facilities. Top hospitals: Lanka Hospitals, Apollo Hospital, Asiri Hospital, Nawaloka Hospital. Some international accreditation. English-speaking doctors. Affordable prices.',
      insurance: 'Not mandatory but highly recommended for expats. Many expats have international health insurance. Local private insurance is also available and cheap. Employer-sponsored insurance is common for professional expats.',
      cost: 'Very affordable. Private consultations $10–30. Procedures and hospital stays cost a fraction of Western prices. Dental work is very cheap. Medical tourism is growing but not as established as in Thailand or India.',
      emergency: '1990 for emergency services. Ambulance quality varies. Private hospitals have their own ambulance services. For emergencies, expats go directly to private hospitals like Apollo or Lanka Hospitals. Medical evacuation to Singapore or India is an option for serious cases.',
      note: 'Healthcare in Colombo has improved but is still developing. The private healthcare sector is growing and several hospitals offer decent care at very affordable prices. Lanka Hospitals, Apollo Hospital Colombo, Asiri Hospital, and Nawaloka Hospital are among the best private hospitals — they have modern facilities and English-speaking doctors. Public hospitals are free but crowded and underfunded — not recommended for expats. For routine care and minor illnesses, private clinics and hospitals in Colombo are fine and extremely cheap. For more complex or serious conditions, some expats prefer to go to Singapore (about 3.5-hour flight) or India for higher-level care. Dental care is very affordable and decent quality. Pharmacies are common and many medications are available without a prescription. The cost of healthcare is one of the lowest in the region. The 2022 economic crisis did affect healthcare — there were shortages of some medications and medical supplies, though the situation has improved. Overall, the private healthcare system is adequate for basic needs and very affordable — but for anything serious, medical evacuation or going abroad may be advisable.',
    },
