    commute: {
      desc: 'Traffic is notoriously bad — Colombo has some of the worst traffic in South Asia. Commutes are slow. Most people use tuk-tuks, buses, or private cars. The city is congested and infrastructure is limited. Living close to work is very important.',
      avg: '45–90 minutes for many commutes. Traffic is very bad during rush hour. Even short distances can take a long time.',
      peak: '7:00–9:00 AM and 5:00–7:00 PM. Heavy traffic on all main roads. Congestion is a major problem. New expressways have helped somewhat.',
      traffic: 'Very Heavy — Colombo has terrible traffic. Rapidly growing vehicle numbers and limited road capacity. Can take hours to go short distances. One of the biggest downsides of living in Colombo.',
      best: 'Living very close to work is essential. Working from home is increasingly common. Traffic worst during monsoon rains. Expressways have improved some commutes but overall congestion is bad.',
      note: 'Traffic in Colombo is notoriously bad — it has some of the worst congestion in South Asia. The city has grown rapidly but road infrastructure has not kept up. Commutes of 45–90 minutes are common even for relatively short distances. During rush hour, the main roads are gridlocked. Rain during monsoon season makes traffic even worse. Most people get around by tuk-tuk, bus, or private vehicle. PickMe/Uber are popular but also suffer from traffic. A light rail/metro system is under construction which should eventually help, but it is years away. Many expats choose to live very close to their workplace to minimize commute time — this is one of the most important factors when choosing where to live. Expressways to the airport and southern Sri Lanka have improved intercity travel, but intracity traffic remains terrible. The airport is about 30–45 km north of Colombo and can take 1–2 hours depending on traffic. Overall, traffic is the biggest downside of living in Colombo.',
    },
    taxes: {
      overview: 'Progressive personal income tax up to 36% (increased during economic crisis). VAT 15%. Moderate to high tax rates. Very low cost of living. Taxes increased significantly in 2023 due to the economic crisis and IMF program.',
      income: 'Progressive personal income tax: up to 36% top rate (increased from 24% in 2023 due to the economic crisis and IMF bailout). Rates were increased significantly to address the fiscal crisis. PAYE tax for employees.',
      capitalGains: 'Capital gains tax: varies by type of asset. Generally 10% for certain capital gains. Some exemptions. Not as straightforward as in some countries.',
      property: 'Property tax: varies by municipality. Generally low. Annual property taxes are minimal compared to Western countries.',
      sales: 'VAT: 15% standard rate (increased during crisis). Some items are exempt or zero-rated. Higher than before the crisis.',
      corporate: 'Corporate income tax: standard rate 30% (increased). Tax incentives for certain industries. Rates were raised during the economic crisis as part of the IMF program.',
      socialSecurity: 'Employees Provident Fund (EPF): employee 8%, employer 12%. Employees Trust Fund (ETF): employer 3%. Total employee contribution 8%, employer ~15%.',
      note: 'Sri Lanka has moderate to high tax rates following significant increases in 2023 as part of the IMF bailout program addressing the economic crisis. The top personal income tax rate was raised to 36%. VAT was also increased to 15%. These tax increases were controversial but necessary for the IMF program. For US citizens working in Sri Lanka, you still need to file US tax returns — the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion and Foreign Tax Credit help reduce or eliminate double taxation. Sri Lanka and the US do not have a tax treaty. Many digital nomads and expats with foreign income may not be tax residents of Sri Lanka depending on their visa and length of stay. The very low cost of living in Sri Lanka still means a high quality of life even with higher tax rates for those earning foreign currency. For locals earning in LKR, the tax increases plus inflation have been very difficult. Overall, taxes have become higher due to the economic crisis but the extremely low cost of living still makes it affordable for expats with hard currency.',
    },
    visa: {
      summary: 'Sri Lanka offers various visa types: ETA (Electronic Travel Authorization) for tourism (30 days, extendable), business visas, work visas, resident visas for investors/retirees, and a digital nomad visa. ETA is easy to get online.',
      tourist: {
        duration: '30 days (ETA — extendable up to 90 days, and then further to 180 days)',
        eligible: 'US citizens and most nationalities can get ETA online before arrival. Very easy. Multiple extensions possible. Tourism-friendly.',
        note: 'Easy for tourism. ETA is obtained online ($50 for 30 days). Can be extended up to 90 days and then up to 180 days. Sri Lanka is very accessible for visitors.',
      },
      digitalNomad: {
        duration: 'Digital Nomad Visa (Sri Lanka Remote Work Visa) — 1 year, renewable',
        income: '$2,000+ USD monthly minimum for individuals; $3,000+ for families',
        desc: 'Sri Lanka offers a remote work / digital nomad visa for those working remotely for foreign companies. Allows stays of up to 1 year, renewable. Requires proof of income and health insurance. Aimed at attracting digital nomads to the island.',
      },
      goldenVisa: {
        duration: 'Resident Visa / Investor Visa — multiple paths available',
        desc: 'Sri Lanka has several residency options: investor visa (minimum investment), retirement visa (minimum deposit), business visa, and employment visa. The Resident Guest Scheme allows long-term residency through investment or fixed deposit. Paths vary and requirements can change.',
        note: 'Sri Lanka has various residency options but no single "golden visa" program. Investor visas require significant investment. Retirement visas require a fixed deposit in a Sri Lankan bank. Requirements and policies can change — especially since the economic crisis. Citizenship is very difficult to obtain. Overall, getting long-term residency is possible but the process can be bureaucratic and requirements may change.',
      },
      work: {
        duration: 'Work Permit + Residence Visa — 1 year, renewable',
        desc: 'Work permit for foreign employees. Employer sponsorship required. Board of Investment (BOI) companies can get work permits more easily. Process involves several government agencies. Can be bureaucratic. Valid for 1 year and renewable.',
        process: 'Employer applies for work permit through the Department of Immigration. Then employee applies for entry visa and residence visa. Medical check may be required. Process takes 1–3 months. Can be bureaucratic. BOI-registered companies have a smoother process.',
      },
      retirement: {
        duration: 'Retirement Visa (Resident Guest Scheme) — 1 year initially, renewable',
        income: '$2,000+ USD monthly remittance OR $10,000+ USD initial deposit + monthly remittance',
        desc: 'Sri Lanka offers a retirement / resident guest scheme for foreigners who want to retire in Sri Lanka. Requires proof of income/financial means. Allows long-term residency. Popular with retirees due to the low cost of living.',
      },
      note: 'Sri Lanka has various visa options and is generally accessible for tourists and expats. The ETA (Electronic Travel Authorization) makes tourist visits very easy — 30 days online, extendable to 90+ days. For longer stays, there are work permits, business visas, retirement visas, and investor visas. The digital nomad visa is a newer option for remote workers. The process can be bureaucratic and requirements can change — especially since the 2022 economic crisis. Some expats do visa runs to neighboring countries (India, Maldives, Thailand) if they cannot get extensions. Citizenship is very rare and difficult to obtain. The immigration system is less organized than in more developed countries — using an agent or lawyer is often helpful. Overall, short-term stays are very easy and long-term residency is possible through various paths but can require some effort.',
    },
