      atms: 'ATMs widely available in Colombo and major cities. Most accept international Visa/Mastercard. Withdrawal limits. Currency devaluation has affected withdrawal limits at times. Cash is still very important.',
      cards: 'Cards accepted at mid-range and up restaurants, hotels, and supermarkets. Cash is still king for small shops, markets, and local vendors. Mobile payments (eZ Cash, mCash) are popular but cards are less universal than in more developed countries.',
      online: 'Improving online and mobile banking. Commercial Bank and HNB have decent digital services. You can do basic transactions online. International transfers are possible but may have restrictions due to currency controls. Digital banking has improved but is not as advanced as in more developed countries.',
      note: 'Banking in Sri Lanka has improved in recent years but is still developing. Commercial Bank of Ceylon and Hatton National Bank (HNB) are the largest and most popular private banks with expats. Sampath Bank is also known for good digital services. International banks like HSBC and Standard Chartered have a presence but with limited retail services. Opening a bank account as a foreigner requires a visa and various documents — requirements vary by bank. The economic crisis of 2022 caused significant challenges: currency controls, import restrictions, and difficulty with international transfers. The Sri Lankan Rupee has depreciated significantly, making the country very cheap for foreigners with hard currency but difficult for locals. Cash is still very important — many small shops and markets only accept cash. Mobile money (eZ Cash, mCash) is popular among locals. For expats, having an international account (Wise, etc.) plus a local account for daily expenses is the best setup. Overall, banking works but is not as advanced as in Thailand or Singapore, and the economic situation has created some challenges.',
