      avg: '15–30 minutes for most commutes within the city. Much shorter than Colombo. Traffic is rarely a major problem.',
      peak: 'Moderate traffic during morning and evening rush hours but nothing like Colombo. The city is compact enough that commutes remain manageable.',
      traffic: 'Light-Moderate — some traffic during peak hours but generally manageable. Much less congested than Colombo. Parking is easier. The hilly terrain can make some routes slower.',
      best: 'Living near the city center is convenient. Walking is possible in some areas. Tuk-tuks are cheap and fast. Working from home is growing. The short commute times are a quality-of-life advantage over Colombo.',
      note: 'Commuting in Kandy is much easier than in Colombo. The city is compact and traffic is generally light to moderate. Most commutes are 15–30 minutes, even across the city. During peak hours there is some congestion, especially near the center and around the lake, but nothing compared to Colombo. Tuk-tuks are the primary mode of transport and are very affordable. Buses cover the city and surrounding areas. The hilly terrain means that walking is not always practical, especially between neighborhoods at different elevations. Kandy is well-connected by train and road to Colombo and other parts of Sri Lanka. The train to Colombo takes about 2.5–3 hours and is scenic. For expats who work in Kandy, commute time is rarely a major concern — one of the advantages of living in a smaller city. Overall, commuting is easy and stress-free compared to the capital.',
