      capitalGains: 'Capital gains tax: varies by asset type. Generally 10% for some gains. Some exemptions. Same as the rest of the country.',
      property: 'Property tax: minimal, varies by local council. Very low compared to Western countries. Cheaper than Colombo due to lower property values.',
      sales: 'VAT: 15% standard rate (same nationwide). Same rates as the rest of Sri Lanka. Increased during the crisis.',
      corporate: 'Corporate income tax: 30% standard rate (same nationwide). Increased during the economic crisis as part of the IMF program.',
      socialSecurity: 'EPF/ETF: employee 8% + employer ~15%. Same system as the rest of the country.',
      note: 'The tax system is the same across Sri Lanka — rates do not differ by city. Personal income tax rates top out at 36% and VAT is 15%. These rates were increased significantly in 2023 as part of the IMF bailout. For US citizens, the same considerations apply: file US returns and use the FEIE and Foreign Tax Credit. The extremely low cost of living in Kandy means that even with these tax rates, expats with foreign currency income can live very well on a modest budget. For locals earning in LKR, the tax increases plus high inflation have been very difficult. Kandy is much cheaper than Colombo — housing, food, and transportation are all significantly cheaper. The overall financial picture for expats in Kandy is very favorable if you have foreign income — the low cost of living far outweighs the tax burden.',
