        pitfalls: [
          { title: 'You cannot work in Greece on FIP — all income must be foreign and passive', desc: 'The FIP visa is specifically for people who are financially independent and do not need to work. You are not allowed to take employment in Greece or even do freelance work for Greek clients. If you want to work remotely for foreign companies/clients, you might be better off with the digital nomad visa instead. Some retirees combine FIP with the Non-Dom tax regime for maximum tax efficiency. Just make sure you choose the right visa category for your actual situation — misrepresenting your income source can cause problems at renewal.' },
          { title: 'Pair FIP with the Non-Dom regime for huge tax savings', desc: 'If you are moving to Greece as a retiree or financially independent person, definitely look into Greece\'s Non-Dom (7-year exemption) tax regime. If you qualify (not tax-resident in Greece for 7 of the past 8 years), your foreign-sourced income is completely exempt from Greek income tax for 7 years. For retirees living on a US pension or investment income, this means you pay zero Greek income tax for up to 7 years — a massive benefit. Combined with the low cost of living and warm climate, this makes Greece one of the most attractive retirement destinations in Europe for Americans.' },
          { title: 'Income proof needs to be clear, consistent, and well-documented', desc: 'Greek immigration will want to see that your income is stable, regular, and sufficient. Having 6–12 months of bank statements showing consistent deposits is better than a one-time lump sum. Pension income from Social Security or a company pension is viewed very favorably because it is guaranteed for life. Investment income may need more documentation. The more clear and consistent your income picture, the smoother your application will go. Get official statements and have them translated/apostilled as needed.' },
        ],
        residencyPath: {
          hasPath: true,
          summary: 'The FIP residence permit is issued for 2 years at a time and can be renewed indefinitely as long as you continue to meet the income and other requirements. After 5 years of continuous legal residence, you can apply for permanent residency. After 7 years of total legal residence, you may be eligible to apply for Greek citizenship (requires Greek language proficiency, integration, and other conditions). The path to permanent residency and citizenship is the same as with other long-term residence permits.',
        },
