    bankOverview: 'Well-developed banking system with many options. US expats often maintain both dollar and peso accounts. Online banking is good and improving. ATM network is extensive.',
    bankReq: 'Passport, valid visa / ACR I-Card, proof of address, proof of income or employment, tax ID (TIN). Dollar savings accounts are popular with expats.',
    bankFees: 'Low fees for basic accounts (many free with minimum balance). ATM fees: ₱10–20 ($0.18–0.36). International transfers have reasonable fees.',
    bankATMs: 'ATMs everywhere. Most accept international cards (Visa/Mastercard). Withdrawal limits: ₱10,000–20,000 per transaction.',
    bankCards: 'Debit and credit cards widely accepted in malls, restaurants, and shops. Cash is still king in markets and small businesses. GCash and PayMaya mobile wallets are extremely popular.',
    bankOnline: 'Good — all major banks have mobile apps. BDO and BPI have the largest networks. GCash is a super-popular mobile wallet (like a Philippine Alipay).',
    bankNote: 'The Philippine banking system is solid and modern. Maintaining both a peso and dollar account is common for expats. BDO and BPI are the most popular. GCash mobile payment is ubiquitous — you can pay bills, buy groceries, send money, and more. Some expat-friendly banks: RCBC, Security Bank, UnionBank.',
