    schoolsLocal: 'Indonesian public schools are free but quality varies and instruction is in Indonesian. National private schools are more affordable. Many expat families choose international schools for quality and English instruction.',
    schoolsTuitionRange: '$10,000 – 40,000+ / year',
    schoolsNote: 'JIS is the most prestigious American/international school — one of the oldest and best in Asia. Tuition is among the highest in Southeast Asia. The British School is another top option. Many schools have long waitlists — apply early. Kemang and SCBD are popular areas for families due to proximity to international schools.',
    commuteDesc: 'Jakarta has some of the worst traffic in the world. Commuting between districts can take 1–2 hours each way during rush hour. The MRT and LRT are improving things but coverage is still limited.',
    commuteAvg: '1–2 hours each way for many commuters. Living near work dramatically improves quality of life.',
    commutePeak: '7:00–10:00 AM and 5:00–8:00 PM. Fridays are especially bad.',
    commuteTraffic: 'Severe congestion — Jakarta is consistently ranked among the most congested cities in the world. The MRT has helped but traffic remains brutal on major roads.',
    commuteBest: 'Live near your workplace! SCBD, Sudirman, and Kuningan are business hubs. Many expats live in Kemang or Menteng and commute to SCBD/Sudirman (20–45 min depending on traffic).',
    commuteNote: 'Traffic is the biggest quality-of-life challenge in Jakarta. Most expats minimize commute by living near work. Many have a car and driver (surprisingly affordable — $200–300/month for a full-time driver). Ojol (motorbike taxi) is faster in traffic but more dangerous. The MRT is expanding and will eventually cover more of the city.',
    netSpeed: 'Decent — 10–100Mbps widely available. Fiber is expanding. Speeds and reliability vary by provider and area. Adequate for remote work.',
    netProviders: ['Telkomsel (IndiHome / first and largest)', 'XL Axiata', 'Indosat Ooredoo', 'Biznet (fiber)', 'First Media'],
    netCost: '$25 – 45 / month for home internet (50–100Mbps)',
    netMobile: '$10 – 20 / month for prepaid data — very affordable',
    netContent: 'Most international sites work. Some content may be blocked by the government (filtered internet). VPNs are commonly used.',
    netVPN: 'VPNs are legal and very commonly used by expats and digital nomads. Often necessary for full access to international content.',
    netReliability: 'Generally reliable in upscale areas. Power outages can happen in some areas (UPS/generator recommended for remote workers). Mobile data is a good backup.',
    taxesOverview: 'Progressive income tax system. Indonesia has a territorial system — tax residents are generally taxed on worldwide income. 2022 tax reform changed the top rate.',
    taxesIncome: 'Progressive from 5% to 35%. Top rate of 35% applies to income above Rp 5 billion (~$316,000) (raised from 30% in 2022 tax reform). Standard for Indonesian tax residents.',
    taxesCapitalGains: 'Generally 20% on capital gains (final tax). Different rates for different asset types.',
    taxesProperty: 'Land and Building Tax (PBB): 0.1–0.2% of assessed value — very low. Property is relatively cheap to own.',
    taxesSales: '11% VAT (PPn) — increased from 10% in 2022, with plans for a further increase to 12%. Some goods and services are exempt or zero-rated.',
    taxesCorporate: '22% standard corporate income tax (reduced from 25%). Companies listed on the stock exchange may qualify for a 3% reduction.',
    taxesSocialSecurity: 'BPJS Kesehatan (health): 5% total (1% employee, 4% employer). BPJS Ketenagakerjaan (employment): ~7.24% employee, ~12.24% employer.',
    taxesNote: 'Indonesia has a moderate tax burden for residents. The 2022 Omnibus Tax Law raised the top rate and increased VAT. US citizens file US taxes and use the foreign tax credit. Indonesia has a tax treaty with the US to help prevent double taxation.',
    visaSummary: 'Indonesia offers various visa options including tourist visas that can be extended, work visas (KITAS), retirement visas, and the new Second Home Visa (2024).',
    visaTouristDur: 'B211A tourist / social visa — 60 days (extendable up to 180 days total)',
    visaTouristEligible: 'US citizens can get visa-on-arrival (35 days) or apply for B211A for longer stays',
    visaTouristNote: 'Visa-on-arrival for 35 days (can be extended once for another 30 days). B211A is better for longer stays (60 days + 4 extensions of 30 days each = 180 days total)',
    visaDNDur: 'B211A is commonly used by digital nomads (60 days + extensions)',
    visaDNIncome: 'No specific income requirement for tourist visa B211A',
    visaDNDesc: 'Indonesia does not have a specific digital nomad visa yet (though one has been discussed). Many digital nomads use B211A social/tourist visas. The Second Home Visa is a better option for long-term stay (5–10 years).',
    visaGoldDur: 'Second Home Visa (2024) — 5–10 years stay',
    visaGoldDesc: 'Indonesia Second Home Visa (Rumah Kedua) launched in 2024. Requires depositing 2 billion Indonesian Rupiah (~$127,000) in an Indonesian bank account. Allows 5–10 year stay. Available for multiple nationalities. Includes the right to work in certain cases.',
    visaGoldNote: 'High deposit requirement but long validity. The deposit must remain in the bank during the stay.',
    visaWorkDur: 'KITAS (Limited Stay Permit) — employer-sponsored, 6 months – 2 years',
    visaWorkDesc: 'Work permit (IMTA) + KITAS (residence permit). Employer must sponsor. Process can be bureaucratic and time-consuming. Common for corporate expats and teachers.',
    visaWorkProcess: 'Employer applies for IMTA work permit with Manpower Ministry, then KITAS with Immigration',
    visaRetDur: 'Retirement KITAS (Lansia) — renewable',
    visaRetIncome: '$2,000/month minimum pension or equivalent savings',
    visaRetDesc: 'Retirement visa for foreigners aged 55+. Requires proof of pension or savings, and a local sponsor. Renewable annually. Popular with retirees, though less famous than Thailand or Philippines retirement programs.',
    visaNote: 'Indonesia visa options are improving. The Second Home Visa (2024) provides a long-term option for those with significant savings. B211A is the go-to for medium-term stays. KITAS is the standard work residence permit. Visa runs and extensions can be done in Jakarta or other major cities. Bali often has slightly different/easier processing for certain visa types.',
