    safetyScore: 65,
    safetySummary: 'Bali is generally safe for travelers and expats. Petty crime like theft can happen but is not a major concern. The biggest risks are scooter accidents, natural disasters (volcanoes, earthquakes), and occasional scams.',
    safetyViolent: 'Low — violent crime against foreigners is very rare. Bali is generally a peaceful, safe place to live.',
    safetyPetty: 'Medium — petty theft, bag snatching (especially from motorbikes), and occasional hotel theft. Keep valuables secure and locked up.',
    safetyNight: 'Medium-high — generally safe at night in tourist areas. Use common sense. Be careful on scooters after drinking.',
    safetyScams: 'Medium — taxi scams, fake tour guides, money exchange scams, and "friendly" strangers. Use Grab, reputable money changers, and book tours through trusted operators.',
    safetyNote: 'Bali is one of the safer places to live in Southeast Asia. The main safety concerns are: (1) scooter accidents — this is by far the biggest risk for expats. Always wear a helmet, drive defensively, and do not drink and drive. (2) Natural disasters — Mount Agung and Mount Batur are active volcanoes. Earthquakes happen occasionally. (3) Petty theft and scams. Use common sense and you should be fine.',
