    safetyScore: 45,
    safetySummary: 'Quito has moderate to high crime. Expats in nice neighborhoods are generally safe but should take precautions. Petty theft is common, and muggings can happen. The city has been improving but still requires vigilance.',
    safetyViolent: 'Medium — violent crime exists, especially in poorer neighborhoods and at night. Expats in good areas are rarely targeted but muggings can happen, especially after dark.',
    safetyPetty: 'High — pickpocketing, phone theft, and bag snatching are common in crowded areas, on public transit, and in tourist areas. Be very careful with your phone and wallet.',
    safetyNight: 'Low–medium — avoid walking alone at night, especially in the Historic Center and less wealthy neighborhoods. Use Uber or registered taxis. Nice neighborhoods are safer but still be cautious.',
    safetyScams: 'Medium — be cautious with taxi scams, counterfeit money, and "friendly" strangers. Use official taxis with meters or Uber.',
    safetyNote: 'Quito is not as safe as Cuenca or other smaller Ecuadorian cities. Petty theft is the main concern — be very careful with your phone, wallet, and bags, especially on public transit and in crowded areas. At night, use Uber or taxi apps instead of walking. The Historic Center is safe during the day but can be dangerous at night. Cumbayá is generally considered the safest area of Quito. Express kidnappings (taxi robberies) used to be a concern but Uber has reduced this risk significantly.',
    hcRating: '7 / 10',
    hcSystem: 'Ecuador has a two-tier healthcare system: public (IESS) and private. Private healthcare in Quito is generally very good — modern facilities, well-trained doctors (many trained in the US or Europe), and extremely affordable.',
    hcPublic: 'IESS (Ecuadorian Social Security Institute) provides public healthcare for workers and retirees. Quality varies — public hospitals can be overcrowded and underfunded. Most expats use private care.',
    hcPrivate: 'Excellent private hospitals: Hospital Vozandes (very popular with expats, English-speaking), Hospital Metropolitano, Hospital de los Valles, Clínica Pichincha. Many doctors speak English and have international training.',
    hcInsurance: 'Private health insurance is very affordable ($40–80/month for comprehensive local coverage). International insurance is also available. IESS is mandatory for workers but voluntary for pensioners.',
    hcCost: 'Extremely affordable. Private doctor visit: $20–40. Specialist: $30–60. Hospital room: $50–120/day. Dental work: $20–50 for cleaning. Medical procedures cost a fraction of US prices.',
    hcEmergency: '911 for emergencies. Private hospital ambulances have better response times. Hospital Vozandes and Hospital Metropolitano have good emergency rooms.',
    hcNote: 'Ecuador is known for having excellent and very affordable healthcare — it is a major draw for expats and retirees. Private care costs a fraction of US prices, and many doctors trained in the US or Europe speak English. Hospital Vozandes is the hospital of choice for most expats. For very specialized procedures, some people go to the US or Colombia (Medellín), but most needs are well covered locally. Dental work, eye care, and cosmetic procedures are especially good value.',
    climateDesc: 'Subtropical highland climate. "Eternal spring" — consistently mild year-round, with temperatures ranging from about 10°C (50°F) at night to 20°C (68°F) during the day. Two seasons: wet (Oct–May) and dry (Jun–Sep).',
    climateSeasons: [
      ['Dry season (Jun–Sep)', '10–20°C', 'The sunniest and driest time of year. Clear blue skies, warm days, cool nights. June–September is the peak tourist season. Perfect for hiking and outdoor activities.', 'Best season'],
      ['Wet season (Oct–May)', '10–19°C', 'Rainy season — especially April–May are the wettest months. Rain usually falls in the afternoon/evening. Mornings are often sunny. Slightly cooler and more cloudy.', 'Green season'],
    ],
    climateBest: 'June–September (dry, sunny, perfect weather)',
    climateWorst: 'April–May (wettest months)',
    climateRain: 'About 1,000–1,200mm annually. More rain in the wet season (Oct–May). The eastern side of the city gets more rain than the western side.',
