    bankOverview: 'Ecuador uses US dollars, so banking is simple for Americans. The banking system is stable and modern. Most banks offer online and mobile banking. International transfers are straightforward since everything is in USD.',
    bankReq: 'Passport, visa / cédula (ID card), proof of address, reference letter (sometimes). Opening an account is relatively easy, especially with residency. Tourists may have limited options.',
    bankFees: 'Low to moderate fees. Many accounts waive fees with minimum balance. ATM fees: $0.50–1.50 at other bank ATMs. International wire transfers in USD are straightforward.',
    bankATMs: 'ATMs are widespread in cities and towns. Most accept international cards. Since it is USD, there are no currency conversion surprises.',
    bankCards: 'Debit and credit cards widely accepted in supermarkets, restaurants, and shops. Cash is still commonly used in markets and smaller businesses.',
    bankOnline: 'Good — all major banks have online and mobile banking. Banco Pichincha and Banco de Guayaquil have the best digital services. You can pay bills, transfer money, and manage accounts online.',
    bankNote: 'Using US dollars is one of the biggest advantages of living in Ecuador — no currency risk, no exchange fees, easy transfers to/from US accounts. Banco Pichincha is the largest and most popular bank. Banco de Guayaquil is another good option. Many expats maintain US bank accounts and use ATMs to withdraw cash, or use services like Wise. Having a local bank account is essential for paying bills, receiving salary, etc. The process of opening an account is straightforward once you have residency (cédula).',
