    bankOverview: 'Modern, well-developed banking system. Three big traditional banks (ING, ABN AMRO, Rabobank) dominate. Digital banks like bunq and Revolut are very popular with expats. Direct debit (automatische incasso) is the standard way to pay bills.',
    bankReq: 'BSN (Burgerservicenummer — social security / citizen service number) is needed to open a full account at traditional banks. bunq and Revolut can be opened without a BSN initially. Passport, proof of address, and residence permit required.',
    bankFees: 'Low fees for basic accounts (€2–5/month for most current accounts). Free withdrawals at your banks ATMs. Fees at other ATMs. International transfers via SEPA are free within the EU.',
    bankATMs: 'ATMs everywhere — called geldautomaat or pinautomaat. Most banks use the same ATM network (Geldmaat). Cash use is declining — the Netherlands is very cashless.',
    bankCards: 'Debit cards are everywhere. The Netherlands is one of the most cashless societies in Europe — many places do not accept cash at all. Apple Pay and Google Pay are widely accepted. iDEAL (bank transfer) is the most common online payment method.',
    bankOnline: 'Excellent online and mobile banking. ING, bunq, and ABN AMRO all have very good apps. Most banking is done digitally. You can pay bills, transfer money, and manage everything from your phone.',
    bankNote: 'Opening a bank account requires a BSN number, which you get after registering at the municipality (gemeente). This can take a few weeks. bunq is a good option for new arrivals because you can open an account with just a passport and get a BSN later. Revolut is also popular. iDEAL is the dominant online payment method — you will need a Dutch bank account for it. Direct debit (automatische incasso) is used for almost all recurring bills — rent, utilities, subscriptions, etc.',
    scores: { costIndex: 30, safety: 75, healthcare: 88, education: 90, internet: 92, walkability: 89, expatCommunity: 92, climate: 68 },
